In article <31D099AD.4AE@cao.mts.dec.com>,
Peter Mayne  <peter.mayne@cao.mts.dec.com> wrote:
> You've got to trust *somebody*. 8-)

As little as possible.

I would prefer, for the sorts of systems that I would want to install C2 on,
that *nobody* logs in with root permissions except from the console. That way
even if the black hats *do* get the root password they can't use it.

I would disable "su". I would make *all* daemons run under a non-root UID. I
would provide a non-root account that had the ability to create users and
reset passwords for non-system users, and do the other things that people
normally do as root. I would remove the scavenger scripts from cron and
replace them with compiled code, and make sure anything run with root
privileges had a statically linked binary.

THEN I would consider the system to be secure and to satisfy the intent of
"C2", because there was no way that a compromised password could be boosted
to root privileges, so your C2 system could actually monitor your "system
administrators and security officers".

For most people, though, C1 is plenty.

