In article <brown.27.000E7777@lincoln.ac.nz>,
	brown@lincoln.ac.nz (Brown, Martyn) writes:
 
> If I decide to go LSM, is it redundant to go to Advfs first, or am I better 
> off installing Advfs, then putting in LSM on top ?

LSM by itself does nothing but group physical disks into logical units
(and other assorted volume management operations) You still need to write
a filesystem on top of the LSM volume in order to use the disk with the
operating system.

> Under what circumstances is each of the following the best choice :
> - Advfs, not LSM ?

This is probably the way to go since Advfs gives you some rudimentary
volume management capabilities. You can't do disk striping without LSM
though (you can only stripe individual files under Advfs).

> - LSM, not Advfs ?

Only if you need a raw disk, or plan to put a UFS filesystem on top of
your LSM volume.

> - Advfs, with LSM on top ?

No such thing. You'd make an LSM volume, then create an Advfs filesystem
on top of that. Of course, you won't be able to remove said LSM volume
since Advfs doesn't recognize LSM device names :)

Good luck,
jonathan
