In "changing colours," M. R. Bray wrote:

     "However I think my problem is a little more fundamental and
     simpler, I just need to know what is the name of the
     scrollbar/pushbutton shadow in the resources file, and which
     resources file it is in - Mwm, DXterm, other ??? Then I can set it
     to my chosen colour. I expect it's something like
     Mwm*scrollbartopshadowcolor: #xxxxxxxxxxxx"

I notice that when I call up the border color changer (by clicking on
the root window), I get a widget that allows me to independently change
the background and shadow colors for the active and inactive window
borders.  When I call up the color changer for the window itself (by
selecting "Options | Window..." from the Session Manager), I just get
to change the background color.  Motif bases the scrollbar shadow color
on the background color; thus, background, top shadow, and bottom
shadow should all be coordinated.  I don't see a convenient way of
independently changing the shadow color.

It occurs to me, though, that the scrollbars for things like DECterm
aren't part of Motif -- they're resources for the applications
themselves.  Sure enough, in the resource file for DECterm
(default.DECterm) there is this line:

   DXterm.main.terminal.topShadowColor:  {some value}

Changing this might work.  (Curiously, it didn't for me, but I didn't
try it aggressively.)  The point is, that the color of the scrollbar
widgets may be controlled by the applications, rather than imposed by
the window manager.

The good news is, if you can get DECterm to pay attention to your
default.DECterm file, you've licked the scrollbar color problem.  The
bad news is, now you've got to find the resources for all your OTHER
applications!

-Good Luck,
