From: hiersekorn@addon.de (Edgar Hiersekorn)
Subject: Re: Advfs vs LSM
Date: 1996/06/28
Message-ID: <31d38c7a.3471944@news>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 162541295
references: <brown.27.000E7777@lincoln.ac.nz>
organization: AddOn Systemhaus GmbH
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

brown@lincoln.ac.nz (Brown, Martyn) wrote:

>Hi,
>
>We have just got our first Alpha (400 4/233).  My first question is :
>Advfs and LSM seem to have a lot of overlapping features, so which to choose ? 
>If I decide to go LSM, is it redundant to go to Advfs first, or am I better 
>off installing Advfs, then putting in LSM on top ?

LSM and AdvFS are on different layers!
AdvFS is a Filesystem, built on top of a disk partition or
a "logical disk" built with LSM (note: AdvFS speaks of Volumes
and means exactly a disk partition or a LSM Volume)

                  -------------------------------------
                  |       Application                 |
                  -------------------------------------
                      Systemcall open(), read(), write()
                                   |
                                   V
                  -------------------------------------
                  |     Virtual Filsystem             |
                  -------------------------------------
                  | UFS | AdvFS | CDFS | NFS | ...    |
                  -------------------------------------


                  -------             -------
                  |Disk |             |logi-|
                  |Par- |             |cal  |   "Volume"
                  |ti-  |             |Disk |
                  |ion  |             |     |
                  -------             -------
                                       /  \
                                      /    \
                                -------    -------
                                |Disk |    |Disk |
                                |Par- |    |Par- |  "Disk"
                                |ti-  |    |ti-  |  usually Part. c
                                |ion  |    |ion  |
                                -------    -------

>Under what circumstances is each of the following the best choice :
>- Advfs, not LSM ?
>- LSM, not Advfs ?
>- Advfs, with LSM on top ?

If you need fast filesystem recovery, online backup,
you need AdvFS.

If you need RAID0, RAID1 or RAID0+1 you need LSM
(AdvFS can only stipe files, not disks)

If you need filesystems bigger than one disk, you
can realize it with LSM or AdvFS:
- LSM makes one volume from 2 or more disks
- AdvFS can be spread over 2 or more volumes
For this feature I prefere AdvFS, because you
cannot (yet) grow the AdvFS if the underlying
volume becomes bigger (by adding a disk).


Edgar Hiersekorn, AddOn Systemhaus, Germany



