From: Patrick O'Rourke USG <orourke@zk3.dec.com>
Subject: Re: why my swap space run low?
Date: 1996/06/28
Message-ID: <31D3DAAB.167E@zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 162562652
references: <4qqbsf$10ap@ctsc.hkbc.hk>
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: CTG	
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1
x-mailer: Mozilla 2.0 (X11; I; OSF1 V4.0 alpha)

Wong Pui Ming wrote:
> 
> lately our osf1 (ver 3.0) always got the message
> swap space is low. But in fact there are not too many processses
> running. I did the swapon -s to see, and got the following
> Could some spot what's going on for me please ?

It appears as if your system is running in 'eager' swap mode.  This
means that for every page of virtual memory allocated, there must
be a corresponding page of swap space to back it up (in the event
the system may need to page it out).  Did you run 'swapon -s' at the
time you were getting the low swap space messages?  Was there an
application attempting to allocate a large region of virtual memory?

As an alternative to adding more swap space, you can run in 'lazy' swap
mode by renaming the symbolic link '/sbin/swapdefault' and then reboot.
In lazy swap mode the system does not reserve swap space until it actually
needs it (i.e. at page out time).  One caveat about running in lazy swap mode
is if you do exhaust your swap space while swapping out a process, that process
will be killed.

Pat

