I have found a mysterious bug with our Vis5D system running
on DEC Alpha systems running OSF1 [specifically Digital
UNIX V3.2C Worksystem Software (Rev. 148)].

Vis5D gets a segmentation fault on entry to a function with
large local arrays.  After the crash dbx says that some
local variables have bad addresses, and these bad addresses
are growing downward in sequence and undoubtedly on the stack.
If I modify the Vis5D source to shorten the large local arrays,
then it gets no segmentation fault.

First I did a 'man cc' and 'man ld' and could not find any
options for controlling stack size.  Then I did 'man -k stack'
and looked at all the pages that mentioned the stack.  The most
useful was 'pthread_attr_setstacksize' which controls stack
size on thread creation (not what I need, since Vis5D uses only
one thread on Alpha and it is created before Vis5D starts running).
However, 'man pthread_attr_setstacksize' says that 'Most compilers
do not check for stack overflow.'  Vis5D is written in C and
presumably the C compiler does not check for stack overflow.

So here is my question: do you know of anything I can do to
control stack size for running Vis5D?  I need a larger stack.

Thank you.

