In article <4qbmut$rpq@web.nmti.com>
 <peter@nmti.com> wrote:
> To be nit-pickingly precise, I want shadowed passwords. Shadowed passwords
> are not a requirement at the "C" level at all, and virtually all modern
> UNIX systems (which approximate "C1") except for Digital UNIX provide them
> as a standard part of the system.

If you want only shadowed passwords, then just turn on only the shadowed
passwords.  Again, shadowed passwords, ACL's, and auditing features are
each individually enabled/disabled.  There is no single switch to 
'enable DEC's "C2" support'. 

Next, on Digital UNIX, shadowed passwords are provided as a standard part
of the system.  No additional fees or licensing required. 

> However it appears that Digital's C2 isn't C2 at all:

Digital's C2-subset provides a superset of those features required for a 
C2 level of security.

> > And the ability to dbx a live kernel or core dump still exists.  The audit
> > system is invisible to everyone other than the admin.  Whatever worked
> > without audit still works with audit. 
> 
> 2.2.2.2 doesn't make any provision for system administrators being immune
> to auditing. In fact, there's explicit language stating that they aren't
> immune to auditing.
> 
> > Sounds like you have an issue with SCO and SecureWare (different newsgroup).
> 
> And NT as well. In fact Digital UNIX is the first supposedly C2 system that
> doesn't at least attempt to prevent system administrators from bypassing
> auditing.

The Orange Book requirement states that system admin actions be auditable.
System admin actions are auditable on Digital UNIX. 

So, if by "immune" you mean that admins are not subject to audit, then you
are mistaken.  You're also mistaken if you are trying to imply that the 
requirements preclude the ability for a sufficiently privileged user 
(such as root) to disable auditing of his actions.  Understand, on UNIX, 
*root is part of the TCB*.

For a sufficiently privileged system admin (such as a root user), you
better expect that *ALL* of your local security features can be bypassed
(and this is not confined to Digital UNIX).  Access decisions are 
obviously different for a root user, and a root user can disable auditing 
of his actions.

If your definition of system administrator is a non-root user with access 
to a restricted, privileged interface for admin tasks, then the admins 
will not be able to bypass normal security features (including audit), 
except as designed into this privileged interface.

> > No.  "Authorization" reflects the implementation of the system security 
> > policy.  A C2 rating requires a set of *features*.  MS-DOS does not have 
> > the *features*, and will not pass C2.
> 
> It appears it's not the only one:
> 
> > There is no "language about logging changes in the TCB" in DOD 5200.28-STD 
> > (the Orange Book).
> 
> 2.2.2.2: "The TCB shall be able to record the following types of events [...]
> actions taken by computer operators and system administrators and/or system
> security officers, and other security relevant events."

And nowhere does this requirement specify logging changes made to the TCB.
It does, however, require logging changes made to objects protected by the
TCB (among other requirements).  Perhaps you should clarify your statements 
here.

Understand, the TCB is the implementation of the security policy.  This
includes the hardware, firmware, and some software.  If you change your
TCB, you have violated the integrity of your TCB, and can no longer
guarantee the correctness of your protection mechanism.  An obvious
example of modifying your TCB is to dbx your kernel (as root) and modify
the logic which performs access checks or audit.  If root invalidates
audit, then it's not reasonable to expect that modification be audited. 
But, (again) *root is part of the TCB*. 

[from another one of peter's postings]:

> In any case, all this "C2" stuff adds extra complexity, and doesn't provide
> a significant amount of extra functionality for most sites... what good are
> logs that are never examined? What *is* useful in the "C2" package are the
> shadow passwords and the ACLs, which don't actually have anything to do with
> C2 certification, and *do* improve security.

A response to this kind of statement requires more information than I care
to provide.  I'm sure a number of introductory computer security texts could
help you out here. 

> Shouldn't there be an option that just turns on shadowed passwords, an
> option that just turns on ACLs, without adding all the auditing and
> changes in traditional UNIX security semantics?

Again, that is *exactly* what has been provided.

And again, Digital UNIX audit changes no system semantics.

lscott



