I am using two alpha 400/233 with Digital Unix 3.2c in a sort of NFS cluster.

This picture might show a more clear situation:


    -------					 -------
   |       |-RZ0				|       |-RZ0
   | AXP1  |-RZ2				|AXP2   |
   |       |-RZ3				|       |
    -------					 -------
    /	(advfs on /dev/rz0a)			/  (advfs on /dev/rz0a)
    /usr (advfs on /dev/rz0g)			/usr (advfs on/dev/rz0g)
    /usr/2giga  (advfs on /dev/rz2c)
    /usr/extra   (advfs on /dev/rz3c)

AXP1 exports /usr/users, /usr/2giga and /usr/extra to AXP2.
AXP2 exports a directory under /usr (/usr/common) to AXP1.

AXP1 mounts /usr/common@AXP2 rw,bg,hard,intr,retrans=16
AXP2 mounts /usr/users,/usr/2giga and /usr/extra rw,bg,hard,intr,retrans=16

AXP1 is a Primary server for a NIS domain. AXP is a slave NIS server.

AXP1 and AXP2 are DNS clients of an Ultrix server (not shown).

The problem is the following:

A user logs into AXP2 successfully and is able to list the content of his
directory (served from AXP1), but when copies a big file from a local disk to e.g.
/usr/2giga, his terminal is almost completely locked while the workstation console
shows a message saying "NFS3 server AXP1 not ready, Still trying...", then "NFS3
server AXP1 OK", then "NFS3 server AXP1 not ready, Still trying..." and so on.

If the file is very long (more than 500Kbytes) you can wait several minutes before
the copy completes, if any.

The same things happen in the reverse case.

I tried to mount the remote directories using NFS2 instead of NFS3 and the
situation is more acceptable, the copy operation proceeds more quickly and the
terminal doesn't stall. 

Any suggestion ? Any explanation ?

Orazio Mistretta



