patteeuw@sys2.ped.pto.ford.com (Jack Patteeuw) writes:

>While chaseing down another problem, a post in a different news group
>indicated that RPC (Remote Procedure Call) required "real" memory for its
>buffers.  By "real" I mean locked down physical memory and **NOT** virtual
>or paged memory.

User level Sun RPC calls and replies use memory that is in part the
user's data space and in part allocated by malloc().  Not locked down.

Kernel level Sun RPC calls and replies (e.g. NFS) use memory from the
kernel memory allocater.  While it's in use, it doesn't get paged out.
When it's free, if the whole page is free, it gets put on the system free
list and is available for any purpose.
