From: nbacon@ibm.net
Subject: Auto template instantiation DEC C++ V5.3-004
Date: 1996/06/20
Message-ID: <4qaukv$pg9@doc.zippo.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 161251966
organization: European Commission
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


DEC C++ V5.3-004 on Digital UNIX V3.2D-1 (Rev. 41)

Here's my experience of auto template instantiation.

Error linking 1st DEC STL example in "Using DEC C++ for Digital UNIX Systems"
section 6.5.

$ cxx testVector.cxx
ld:
Unresolved:
__cxx_gem_dispatch

This symbol is in module cxx_exc.o of library /usr/lib/libcxx.a, which is
supposed to be linked in by default.
From nm:

Name                                    Value        Type       Size
__cxx_gem_dispatch               | 0000000000004496 | T | 0000000000000008

$ ar x /usr/lib/libcxx.a cxx_exc.o
$ cxx testVector.cxx cxx_exc.o
$ a.out
0 1 2 3 

Now its ok! Strange because "cxx -v testVector.cxx" shows that these libraries
are linked in: -lcxxstd -lcxx -lexc -lots -lc. Extra libraries specified on the
command line go in front of these.

--------------

Then I tried compiling the project I'm working on and had far worse problems.
The project is split into 3 parts each in its own directory (we make a library
from each of the first 2 parts and link them with the .o's from the 3rd part).
All 3 parts use templates and some are in common between the parts. To share
the template instantiations, I pass the same '-ptr templateRepositoryDir' args
to cxx when compiling each part. It compiles successfully, but I think slower
than with manual instantiation (pragma define_template). Just before linking
cxx compiles all the template code it has previously produced in
templateRepositoryDir. This is hideously slow (about 4 hours for me)!
Compiling with -g I ended up with over 100Mb of template .o's. ld fell over
with 'cannot malloc'. When I tried to build a library of the template .o's,
ar fell over with 'bad file number'. I did succeed in stuffing all the template
.o's into 5 different libraries, but ld still fell over. Then I recompiled with
-O2 and managed to build an executable.

Conclusion:
Auto template instantiation works ok for baby test programmes, but isn't yet
good enough for real work. One day it will be a great feature and we will
really need it to use the STL!



