In article <4qc5g1$12s@d2.tufts.edu>, jrozes@tcs.tufts.edu wrote:
| In article <4qbmut$rpq@web.nmti.com>,
|         peter@nmti.com (Peter da Silva) writes:
| 
| > To be nit-pickingly precise, I want shadowed passwords. Shadowed passwords
| > are not a requirement at the "C" level at all, and virtually all modern
| > UNIX systems (which approximate "C1") except for Digital UNIX provide them
| > as a standard part of the system.
| 
| I'm kinda surprised that after _years_ of people moaning about the lack
| of shadow passwords (myself included), nobody has bitten the bullet and
| ported the public-domain shadow utilities to DUX.

I didn't do it myself, so don't know the details, but we replaced DEC's
passwd code with a shadow passwd implementation.  Where on Ultrix
we had to replace the relevant parts of libc.a and rebuild the relevant
applications, on Digital UNIX we do it with shared libraries.

| OTOH, the best thing to do would be for all of us to write/call/email
| Digital and tell them that Enhanced Security is a bear, BSD security
| is an oxymoron, and that shadow passwords should be standard/mandatory
| for base security.

It makes more sense for DEC to provide this, for sure, than for
everyone to hack it in independently.   We just couldn't wait for a fix,
and didn't want C2 - like the man said, "don't want C2, just shadow
passwords".  Again, I wasn't directly involved, but we were not able
to make C2 work here in a way we could live with.

Just a fine point, though - while BSD 4.2 didn't have shadow passwords,
it's not like everyone else did at that point.  Later BSD versions do have a
shadow password scheme, and in fact that's what our system is based on,
so here the term "BSD security" would have to mean shadow passwords!
So we wouldn't like to think of it as an oxymoron.  As I understand it we
changed the dbm database setup to cut down on the frequency of required
rebuilds, but it's basically per BSD 4.3-Tahoe - which is in general licensed
code, though for all I know its shadow passwd stuff may be public domain.

