In article <31C976A3.446B@nautique.epm.ornl.gov>,
Lawrence Macintyre  <lpz@nautique.epm.ornl.gov> wrote:
> On ANY C2 system, if you can't figure out how to bypass auditing when
> you have root privs, then you don't know enough about that system.

That may be true. Which means that you don't have a "useful" C2 system
without administrative tools that let you do most of the "root" things
without "root". I think that for all practical purposes a sufficiently
obfuscated TCB will serve the purpose. Given sufficiently competant
administrators and sufficient time you can probably get around that,
but the main virtue I see is logging the actions of intruders who have
cracked root but don't grok the TCB well enough to hide their actions.

In any case, all this "C2" stuff adds extra complexity, and doesn't provide
a significant amount of extra functionality for most sites... what good are
logs that are never examined? What *is* useful in the "C2" package are the
shadow passwords and the ACLs, which don't actually have anything to do with
C2 certification, and *do* improve security.

Don't you see something wrong with this picture?

Shouldn't there be an option that just turns on shadowed passwords, an
option that just turns on ACLs, without adding all the auditing and
changes in traditional UNIX security semantics?
