Peter da Silva wrote:
> In any case, all this "C2" stuff adds extra complexity, and doesn't provide
> a significant amount of extra functionality for most sites... what good are
> logs that are never examined? What *is* useful in the "C2" package are the
> shadow passwords and the ACLs, which don't actually have anything to do with
> C2 certification, and *do* improve security.
> 
> Don't you see something wrong with this picture?
> 
> Shouldn't there be an option that just turns on shadowed passwords, an
> option that just turns on ACLs, without adding all the auditing and
> changes in traditional UNIX security semantics?

This is on my v4.0 system.

user root				user mayne
# echo hello world > file.txt
# chmod 700 file.txt
					> cat file.txt
					cat: cannot open file.txt
# setacl -u user:mayne:r-- file.txt
					> cat file.txt
					hello world
					> echo xyz >> file.txt
					ksh: file.txt: cannot create
# setacl -u user:mayne:rw- file.txt
					> echo xyz >> file.txt
					> cat file.txt
					hello world
					xyz
# setld -i|grep C2
OSFC2SEC400                     C2-Security (System Administration)
OSFXC2SEC400                    C2-Security GUI (System Administration)
#

Note that ACLs work as expected, even though C2 isn't even installed.

As for C2 without auditing, this is on the same system, after installing the C2 
subsets, although roughly the same would happen on pre-v4.0 systems:

# secsetup
.
.
Enter system security level(BASE ENHANCED ?)[ENHANCED]:
ENHANCED security level will take full effect on the next system reboot.
.
.
.
Do you wish to run the audit setup utility at this time(yes no ?)[no]:

Press return to continue:
#

So you don't need to turn auditing on when you turn C2 on.
