In article <4qa3vp$lpg@web.nmti.com>, Peter da Silva <peter@nmti.com> wrote:

>We don't have access to the source of all the apps, so we need to
>automatically update the routing tables so that packets for X.X.3 get
>routed to X.X.1. I originally thought that just running routed -q
>would do this... apparently not.

Well, no. The problem is that you've got x.x.3.1 talking to x.x.3.2,
not x.x.1.2. So when x.x.3.0 goes down, now it x.x.3.1 would have
to see a route to x.x.3.2 on the x.x.1.1 interface, which is not
going to work if it also knows that it's directly connected to
x.x.3.0 through another card. And regardless, you probably want to
make sure that traffic for x.x.3.2 goes to that machine directly,
and doesn't go to another machine advertising itself as a gateway
and then get routed across what remains of the x.x.3.0 network (if
that even still works).

>We can detect when the lan goes down and do the switch ourselves, but
>is there any existing software that does this automatically? If there's
>some option to gated or routed that would handle it that would be
>optimal.

Presumably you want a fast cutover, so what about doing something
like this? Set up gated on all the systems, and have each system
broadcast a host route through the x.x.1.x interface to its x.x.3.x
address. This gives your machine lower priority alternate routes
to x.x.3.x machines through the x.x.1.x network.

Now, when your x.x.3.x network goes down, detect it through whatever
means you like and ifconfig the interface down. (Gated will decide
an interface is down anyway when it stops receiving routing protocol
packets on that interface, but this can take a couple of minutes.)
Now gated should install the lower priority routes to the x.x.3.x
machines through the x.x.1.x network into the kernal routing table,
and connectivity should be restored.

If you've got questions about getting host routes broadcast by
gated, drop me a note; it was a bit tricky when I was doing it,
and I can't remember off-hand what I had to do.

cjs
