From: pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN)
Subject: dUnix 4.0 upgrade (long)
Date: 1996/06/22
Message-ID: <4qha7u$tgq@tom.amherst.edu>
X-Deja-AN: 161579367
organization: Amherst College, Amherst MA, USA
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


I've just completed several upgrades/new installations of dUnix
4.0. In general the experience was only slightly less pleasant that
crushing one's hand in a car door.

The first thing is that the new installation takes up a bunch of room.
This can be particularly a problem on the root directory, which has
been slowly growing for the last couple of upgrades.  I spend more
time cleaning out space that actually running the upgrades.  This was
made worse in that you have to get about ten to fifteen minutes into
the upgrade before it bombs out on you and its reporting is such that
just freeing up as much space as it asks for doesn't necessarily mean
it will run next time.  The first time it asked for 9 megs more, but I
had to finally free up 25 megs before it was happy.  That's a lot of
interations.  You wouldn't believe some of the ugly things I did in
order to get this to run.  In generally, the era of 64 meg root
partitions is probably over.

Upgrading a system with graphics capability proved effectively
impossible.  I knew I was in trouble when the manual said I should see
the log in dialog box, and I was looking at console text.  What I got
was inconsistent results everytime I tried to boot, including a lot of
memory faults and other nasty sounding systems stuff.  For awhile I
got the login box, and it would accept my login, but then just give me
another login box.  I worked at that and managed to get back to no
login box.  Then out of disgust I powered down the machine, and it
came up like a champ.  Except that it crapped out during the night and
I couldn't even get back to the login box again.  I finally said to
hell with it and did a base install.  This wasn't as bad as it sounds;
this was the system I use for administration so there wasn't much on
it, and much that was would be replaced or upgraded with 4.0.  It's
worked since then, except for one crash back into single user mode (I
was trying to run Network Save and Restore - see below).

Upgrading a system without graphics was a lot easier, once you found
the space.  The only problem I've really had is with LAT which doesn't
want to recognize the additional service names I have. (Redoing LAT
setup took care of the problem). This was a local hassle because we've
set the system up to accept a rather generic name, unix.amherst.edu,
so that people can put it into scripts and like and have it move to
which ever system we made primary.  (One system I tried to upgrade
like this finally required a complete install, but the to honest it
was more my mistakes (one of those ugly things I mentioned earlier got
me) than a problem with the upgrade.  Fortunately it our news-server
so recovery was fairly simple - except that the news software itself
was uncooperative and forced me to rebuilt the history db, which took
about 16 hours).

A new install without graphics went easily, although again it had
problems with LAT.  This system isn't even available to users yet so
it hasn't reallly been stressed yet.  In general, if you got the space
it should come up nicely if you walk through all the configuration
routines.

A new install with graphics - which I did when I gave up upgrading my
graphics system - is kind of neat with all the graphic interfaces.
The problem is that sometimes it takes a bit to realize what's being
asked for or where you have to go to set something up.  I suspect
that's mostly an artifact of my experience with the non-graphic
install/upgrades.  I keep looking for a command prompt somewhere.

As for the new graphic interface, I'm going to have to hold off of
most judgements since my experience is mostly limited to working with
the upgraded system which was so unstable I can't tell when the
problem was with the interface and when with the system.  You had
better plan on spending some time learning the new interface, it's
very different.  When it was first up I had the neat experience of it
substituting white for a colored background on an application
which wouldn't have been bad except that my text had always been
white.  The result was a very Zen interface. I still haven't figured
out how to adjust the fonts to my liking - for awhile I was actually
starting dxsession and running terminal windows from it.  And I
haven't been able to figure out how to adjust the icon titles and
Window titles for my terminal sessions which I need since I normally
keep windows going on between four and six machines at the same time.
I think the use of workspaces has the potential to make life easier
for those of use who routinely work on multiple nodes at the same
time, but the implications of working mostly from the desktop rather
than terminal windows have not completely revealed themselves to me yet.

If you use Network Save and Restore, you're in for some additional
good times.  I flat out couldn't get it to run without reinstalling
the server - which the upgrade does appear to warn you about.  The
reinstall, however, had two problems.  First, it wrote over some
critical files and apparently made past backups unbrowsable.  I
probably could recover from this, but I had copied everything critical
to another directory and just put it back when needed and that appears
to have made it happy.  The other problem is that the new install is
using a different alias for the server machine than the old, causing
some confusion.  Once I realised this, and moved some files from
/nsr/index/old_name to /nsr/index/new_name, it was a lot happier.

BTW, any idea why this came packaged with old versions of Netscape and
GNU emacs?  I can understand Netscape, it's upgrade is relatively new,
but Emacs 19.29 has been about for almost a year.

--

Paul Chapin
Unix Systems Manager
Amherst College




