Thomas Leitner wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> I would like to have an AdvFS system only. I've already converted my
> /home and /usr partitions to AdvFS without troubles. Now i'd like
> to convert the root paritition to AdvFS as well and put it into
> the same domain as the /usr partition.
> 
> Question: How do I do that? Anyone done that before?
> 
> I supposed I do it like that:
> 
> 1.) Make a backup of everything.
> 2.) Have a OSF/1 boot CD ready and check if I can boot from it
>     and access the root partition! Also verify that vrestore
>     works when booted from the CD.
> 3.) vdump the / partition to an empty tape.
> 4.) Boot from the CD and look if I can access the backup.
> 5.) addvol the / device and mkfset a root file set
> 6.) modify fstab to re-mount it
> 7.) restore the tape.

You'll have to use disklabel to write an AdvFS boot block to the disk. (You'll 
have to do a MAKEDEV first, of course.)

Use mkfdmn -r to specify the root domain.

Put / in its own domain.

You'll have to create /etc/fdmns on the system disk by hand. mkfdmn will create 
the correct pointers, but in the in-memory filesystem when you're booted from 
CD.

Modify fstab *after* you've restored from tape.
-- 
