-- M. van Wijk wrote:
> 
> Do priorities work under OSF pthreads? I get the impression that they are
> not implemented. Setting the schedule priority higher or lower dowsn't
> work in the three policies (SCHED_RR, SCHED_FIFO and SCHED_OTHER).
> 
> What's wrong? (probably me)
> 

Yes, they do work, and you haven't given enough information (here or in
comp.programming.threads) to determine what you're doing, what you expect to
happen, or what is happening. Here's my reply from comp.programming.threads,
just to avoid leaving this loose end.

M. van Wijk wrote:
> I have created a thread and it works fine. That's nice but not nice
> enough. I want to set the scheduling and priority as well. But every time
> I set the priority of a thread higher the pthread_create fails. Setting
> the priority lower works fine though.
>
> As workaround I've set the priority of the main thread lower. Like this:
>
> pthread_setscheduler(pthread_self(), SCHED_RR, PRI_RR_MIN);
>
> This works fine, but is main also executed as a thread on all platforms?
>
> Allow me to correct myself, setting the priority lower doesn't even work.
> Are priorities implemented at all in OSF? In case you are wanedring what
> kind of system I'm working on:
>         OSF1 V3.2 214 alpha
>
> But if priorities are not implemented wouldn't it be much better to define
> PRI_policy_MIN = PRI_policy_MAX?
>
> I think threads are severely crippled without a propper priority control.
>
> Marc van Wijk
> wijk@cs.utwente.nl
> wijk@pegasus.epsrit.ec.org
> m.vanwijk@student.utwente.nl

I don't know anything about your program or your problem, and it may well be
that your application truly requires priority control and that you understand
how to use it correctly. However, as a general statement, threads are
certainly not in any respect "crippled" without priority control. Most
programs gain nothing from priority control except complication. And the vast
majority of people who want to use priority control on a timesharing system
don't know what they're doing (and won't accomplish what they intend).

Now, to your question. Or at least, as well as I can address your question
without more information. First off, prior to Digital UNIX 4.0, Digital UNIX
(and its old name, "DEC OSF/1") implemented what POSIX calls "system
contention scope". That means that all threads you create in a process are
directly represented as kernel scheduling entities ("Mach threads"). The
scheduling policy and priority you specify for those threads affects how they
are scheduled against ALL other threads on the system.

Clearly, it is not reasonable to allow a non-privileged user to create, for
example, a compute-bound thread that will lock out all other activity in other
processes (and even inside the kernel).

The default priority of a thread is 19 (in POSIX priority terms, which is very
different from the traditional "UNIX priority" controlled by nice & friends).
So when you create a thread set to priority 20, it locks out all normal user
process activity. If you go any higher than that, you start locking out kernel
activity.

From that perspective, it may not be surprising to anyone that it requires
privilege (in specific, root access) to set a thread's priority higher than
19. SCHED_FIFO threads only get to go up to 18, because they aren't timesliced
against other threads at the same priority.

For somewhat arbitrary historical reasons, the priority range symbols were
defined so that the "mid point" of the SCHED_FIFO and SCHED_RR priority ranges
would yield the highest priority to which you could set the thread without
privilege. That is, 18 for SCHED_FIFO and 19 for SCHED_RR.

Now, when you say "setting the priority lower doesn't even work", exactly what
do you mean? What did you do, and what happened as a result? "ps xm -O psxpri"
will show you the POSIX priority for all your threads (a "non-threaded"
process is really just a single thread).

Prior to DEC OSF/1 3.0 (e.g., 2.x), the "realtime kernel" was an optional
feature; and without it, setting POSIX priorities was impossible. Trying to
set any POSIX priority would fail with ENOSYS when the realtime kernel wasn't
installed. DEC OSF/1 3.0, however, made the "realtime kernel" support
standard, so setting priority will always be possible -- as long as you're
running as root or using "approved" priority values. Certainly setting the
SCHED_RR/PRI_RR_MIN will succeed. Might well not do what you want, however,
since PRI_RR_MIN is lower than 19, which means you are running at lower
priority than Jane Schmoe's shell (and compiler, editor, etc.)

In Digital UNIX 4.0, POSIX threads are strictly "process contention scope".
You can set any POSIX priority without privilege -- but none of them mean
anything against threads in other processes. In an update to 4.0, we'll be
adding support for the pthread_attr_setscope() attribute, so you can
explicitly create threads in system contention scope when you need them: and
those threads will again require root privilege to set priorities higher than
19 (18 for SCHED_FIFO).

