In article <4p9kis$6sg@netnews.upenn.edu>, ggrevera@gradin.cis.upenn.edu (George Grevera) writes:

>Hi.  I seem to have tons of swap space but can't malloc a measly
>135M.  The particulars are 96M of physical memory on a DEC 3000
>running OSF1 V3.0.  I don't have a /sbin/swapdefault file.
>
> [ swapon -s and sample program deleted ]
>
>I can't understand why this fails.  Thanks for your help.

There are a variety of per-process and system parameters that
limit the amount of virtual memory a process can have.  Some
shells implement a per-process limits, but such shell typically
offer built-in commands to control the limits.  The C-shell
for example has the limit built-in command.

There are system wide per-process limit.  Once upon a time these
were controlled by the parameters maxssiz, maxdsiz and maxtsiz
in the configuration.  I haven't paid close enough attention,
but these may haved move to sysconfigtab and are thus easier
to change.  There is also probably a system wide virtual memory
limit.  How to raise these (and the per-process default) should
be documented in the system management documentation.

>George
