newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


When we make a full backup (level 0) of our AdvFS partitions,
vdump touches some of the files so that their ``last read'' dates
(stat(2)'s st_atime) are updated to the time of the backup.

That causes problems with software that looks at this date with
the expectation that it's a meaningful user access - in particular,
/bin/login looks at /usr/spool/mail/$USER, and fails to mention that
people have new mail, because it's deceived about the last time they
looked.

We think vdump can back files up without this filesystem read
access, but it's going through the filesystem when there might be
buffered data. Very old files don't get ``read'', only relatively newly
modified files (even at level 0).  On the computer we've been looking
at, it's reading files that are a couple of weeks old, and one theory
is that it's anything modified since the last reboot.

What I'm hoping is that there's something we can do to synch things
for vdump, short of a reboot.  I've tried "sync", but with no joy.
Obviously we can't hope for completely reliable backups with the
filesystem active - the goal is just to update vdump's notion of
possibly active files.

Digital UNIX 3.2D.  Here's a df of the filesystem, which to complicate
matters is also NFS mounted to many other hosts.  Cluster has 17K users.
$ df -k /usr/spool/mail
Filesystem       1024-blocks        Used       Avail Capacity  Mounted on
mail_domain#sy29     3910032     1782082     2112400    46%    /sy29

Thanks,
