>this changed in Digital Unix 4.0?

I don't know about 4.0, but there's something here I didn't quite
understand about your comment.  No, you cannot swap to a regular file
under 3.2, but this only matters if you've already allocated all your
disk space to file systems (or database raw partitions) and have no
money left to buy another disk.

Buy a disk, and possibly a SCSI cable.  Check SCSI addresses, shut the
system down, connect the disk, power back up.  Partition the disk as
desired.  Add an entry in /etc/fstab for your new swap partition on the
new disk, and then use the appropriate swapon command.

(If boss won't pay up, ask boss how much money in lost business it will
cost if you must reinstall the OS and the system is down for, say, 1
whole day.  Then compare this figure against the measly price of the
disk & cable!)

(Also, putting swap on multiple disks can be a real performance bonus!)

