In article <4ps3vr$52q@clem.mscd.edu>,
Stephen L. LaBelle <labelles@clem.mscd.edu> wrote:
> Hi,
  
> They work just fine. The problem is that the quota's are not reenabled
> at boot time. A bug or a problem? We noticed this and called DEC support
> they sent us instructions on added the proper startup script. It goes in
> to /sbin/init.d

While it's true that there exists no /sbin/init.d/vquota script the
/sbin/init.d/quota script can be modified to do the analogous thing for
ADVFS quotas.  In any case, this isn't the problem that the Paul is running
into. 

> PAUL D. CHAPIN (pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu) wrote:
> : Do these do anything?  I just found multiple users with multiple
> : files, each exceeding their hard quota limit.  Now I could understand
> : how an ftp process, once started, could happily ignore the quota, but
> : it looks like it promptly let me download another file even after the
> : quota was blown.

There are multiple problems with ADVFS quotas.  One is the `random lack of
enforcement', which you have noticed.  Even worse, we often would get users
that are over quota on a filesystem where they have no quota and have no
files.

I reported a problem with ADVFS quotas on the osf-managers mailing list
several months ago.  Since then additional people have reported the same
problems that our site has experienced.  I opened call number C960116-2017
with Digital at that point.  It was suggested by the support person I spoke
with that we avoid the `-a' and `-v' options to vquotaon &/| vquotacheck.  
Do something like:

                        for i in `/usr/bin/grep 'rq,userquota' /etc/fstab | /usr/bin/grep 'advfs' | /usr/bin/grep -v '^#' | /usr/bin/awk '{ print $2 }'`
                        do
                                /usr/sbin/vquotacheck -u $i
                        done

to check quotas, and the analogous action to enable quotas with vquotaon.

This has helped some; the other thing you should look at is installing the
consolidated ADVFS patch (OSF350-163, currently); that will fix some of the 
other problems with ADVFS and vquotas.

Tim
