We are attempting to build an application that uses:

1) A C++ Class Library
2) An ORB
3) An OODB

The C++ Library is a 64 bit application.
The ORB is a 64 bit application.
The OODB is a 32 bit application.

Compiling and linking with the OODB .so requires the -xtaso_short
compiler flag and the -taso ld flag.  The application will compile and
link successfully, however, it seems that using these flags when linking
against the 64 bit .so files for the C++ Library and ORB library is the
reason we are experiencing runtime SEGV's.

Is this the square peg/round hole problem?  Can we not compile
-xtaso_short and link -taso and expect to be able to run when linked
against a 64 bit .so?  Is this a situation where all of the 32 bit code
must be in the address space of one process and the 64 bit code must be
in the address space of another process?  I know it probably is, but has
anybody worked around this successfully?

