Scenario: A number of Alpha-based servers and workstations, each with
two ethernet cards, running between two parallel redundant lans:


	X.X.3 --------------------------------------------------
		  | X.X.3.1      | X.X.3.2      | X.X.3.3
		  |              |              |
		+---+          +---+          +---+
		|   |          |   |          |   |
		+---+          +---+          +---+
		  |              |              |
		  | X.X.1.1      | X.X.1.2      | X.X.1.3
	X.X.1 --------------------------------------------------

We have applications running on the primary LAN (X.X.3), but if the LAN
fails we want the packets to all get routed over the backup LAN (X.X.1).

We don't have access to the source of all the apps, so we need to
automatically update the routing tables so that packets for X.X.3 get
routed to X.X.1. I originally thought that just running routed -q
would do this... apparently not.

We can detect when the lan goes down and do the switch ourselves, but
is there any existing software that does this automatically? If there's
some option to gated or routed that would handle it that would be
optimal.
