In article <31C501DA.2CBD@ttown.apci.com>, "George S. Witmer" <witmergs@ttown.apci.com> writes:
> the CDE login program dtlogin will not authenticate a user...
> We have been able to login via an xterm windows and then 
> start CDE from the command line provided we are not already running a 
> window manager, but this is a rather ugly kludge that our users won't 
> tolerate.

I came across a similar (if not the same) problem some time ago - I upgraded
from 3.2C to 3.2D-1 and a previously working CDE installation started
disallowing all logins except root. Here's what I got back from Digital:

  I have heard that a patch (for 3.2C) included in 3.2D has broken
  dtlogin and that it has been been fixed for the 4.0 release of the
  OS.  You might try calling your support centre and asking if there
  is a patch but since the version of CDE is not officially supported
  prior to release of the version shipping with 4.0 of the OS, response
  may not be the same as were it supported fully

I then received a work-around which is rather ugly but does work:

  I experienced this problem just last week. I got a new Alphastation
  preloaded with v3.2c. First thing I did was apply the latest dU
  patch kit which brought it to v3.2d. Then I installed CDE from
  the v3.2c CD and got the exact problem you describe. My solution
  is more of a workaround, but it did the trick.

  The /usr/sys/BINARY/sys_sysinfo.o seems to be the problem. Applying
  the patchkit added an new version that breaks CDE in this manner. I
  had the original file as the patchkit renamed it for me... so I
  copied this original sys_sysinfo.o back into place and relinked
  the kernel and CDE behaved.

  Here are the checksums for the old, working one and the new, non-working one:
  $ sum /usr/sys/BINARY/sys_sysinfo.o
  59854     13 sys_sysinfo.o
  $ sum /usr/sys/BINARY/sys_sysinfo.o.new
  12739     13 sys_sysinfo.o.new

Many thanks to Matt Cheek for the work-around.

Hope this helps,

Mark Parsons
