the terminfo database, so you'll have to modify the system terminfo
database or build your own.

--

dphan@alpha.comsource.net (David Phan) writes:
>Help!  I am a newbie to xterm. For some reason, the [PageUp]
>and [PageDown] keys don't work when I am editing files with
>vi or PICO. It works fine when I switched to DECterm.

DECterm uses the vt200 terminfo entry which includes the following
definitions.

	kdo=\E[29~, kich1=\E[2~, knp=\E[6~, kpp=\E[5~, khlp=\E[28~,

Add these (at least knp and kpp) to the xterm entry in your xterm
terminfo source file, /usr/lib/terminfo/xterm.ti.  Then run tic
(terminfo compile) on the xterm.ti file.  If your updating the system
terminfo database, remember to "chmod -R a+rX /usr/lib/terminfo/x"
after you tic, else others may not be able to access the database.

The terminfo database consists of binary files stored in a shallow
directory tree, one file per termtype: the vt100 entry is
$TERMINFO/v/vt100, xterm is $TERMINFO/x/xterm--see a pattern? :-)
TERMINFO would be an environment variable specifying the path to your
terminfo database; the default path, /usr/lib/terminfo, is used if you
have no TERMINFO variable set.

--
Re: vi's mapping of npage to ^F, etc.

This automapping is not documented in the ex or vi man pages and
it doesn't appear possible to remap these keys.  You can map the
function keys, though.  I map the keys to change the shiftwidth
and autoindent options.

	map #1 :set ts=8 sw=4^M
	map #2 :set ts=5 sw=5^M
	map #3 :set noai^M
	map #4 :set ai^M

I usually work in states 2 and 4, but I switch to 1 and 3 when working
with tab aligned files or when I cut and paste from one xterm to
another.  Those are literal carriage returns that I put in my .exrc.
Each #<digit> in the map must be matched by a kf<digit> in your terminfo
entry.

Paul Fardy
