In article <Pine.A32.3.93.960605112455.39137A-100000@spain.endicott.ibm.com> Steve Thompson <v2pen01@spain.endicott.ibm.com> writes:
>I'm looking for a method to read the hw cycle counter under DEC Unix; C
>preferred but assembler OK. Alternatively, some way to read the current
>time to a resolution of at least a microsecond. I'd appreciate a pointer
>to either of these; TIA.

Create rpcc.s with this in it:
        .globl  ReadPCC
        .ent    ReadPCC,0
ReadPCC:;
        .frame  $30,0,$26
        rpcc    $0
        ret     $31,($26)
        .end    ReadPCC


Call it from C like so:

        unsigned int p;
	unsigned int ReadPCC();

        p = ReadPCC();

works for me, anyway.

-Jeff

P.S.: Digital UNIX V4.0 will return microsecond resolution
from gettimeofday(), *if* you rebuild the kernel using the
MICROTIME configuration option.  (Sorry, I haven't tried this
myself so I can't provide full instructions yet.)



