X-Deja-AN: 158996111
references: <31851EDB.68AD@mtu.cl> <31981144.6B5D@cao.mts.dec.com> <4nft2t$8v6@web.nmti.com> <199605281708.NAA12062@orb.nashua.nh.us>
organization: Network/development platform support, NMTI
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


In article <199605281708.NAA12062@orb.nashua.nh.us>,
 <scott@Orb.Nashua.NH.US> wrote:
> Shadow passwords and ACL's are provided to satisfy market requirements, 
> not C2 compliance.  Both shadow passwords and ACL's can be selectively 
> enabled/disabled.

But not without enabling C2. I want these features. I don't want C2.

> pjd> The biggest problem with C2, though, is 2.2.2.2. Any system
> pjd> that satisfies 2.2.2.2 is inherently undebuggable. For
> pjd> software development environments this is a disaster waiting
> pjd> to happen.

> 2.2.2.2 is audit.  Audit and system "debuggability" are completely 
> orthogonal.

Nope.

If you have an auditable system, that means there are no backdoors that
allow you to fly "under the radar" to bypass the auditing.

When you have a system problem, like a corrupted file system, you often
need to operate on the raw bits to debug and fix the problem.

But you can't do that, because that would also allow you to bypass the
auditing. So they either have to disable access to the raw bits, or they
have to obfuscate the audit files and TCB to try and keep you from
figuring out how to do it. So if your TCB database is clobbered you're
out of luck... time to drop back to backups. IF you can even restore
that stuff (if they're not paranoid enough to encode timestamps so that
backups are a loss).

I've been through this problem over and over again with Secureware's C2
enhancements in SCO Open Desktop. C2 auditing requirements are sufficiently
strong that you have to break "root". Which is fine for military
installations but a waste of time for 99% of the systems out there that
just need shadow passwords.

> The requirement for audit trail protection at the C2 level (and through
> the A1 level) is that the audit trail be protected from "unauthorized"
> access.  This means system administrators, as root, can legitimately
> modify the audit trail. 

I don't believe that this interpretation of the word "authorized" is adequate
or else you can simply say "everyone's authorized to do anything without
logging" and get your MS-DOS system to pass C2. If this was adequate there
would be no need to include language about logging changes in the TCB, since
only root can do that and by your reasoning root is "authorized" to do
anything without being logged.
