You're not understanding what I'm saying.  We have numerous 8-bit
color displays (TGA on Alphas, cfb, sfb on DECstations) that do not
exhibit the problem than I'm complaining about.  Let's see if I can
explain this better:

The expected behaviour from a private colour map is to make the window
in focus legible, and all other windows illegible.  This is NOT what
is happening.  BOTH the window with focus & those without are grossly
illegible.  The netscape window becomes illegible abruptly, only after
the colormap is erroneously altered when the pointer is moved over a
hotlink and/or embedded image.  I'm NOT complaining that netscape
doesn't look as good in 2^8 colors as it does in 2^16 or 2^24 colors.

The sequence of events goes as follows:

fire up netscape -install (let's say were using 2.02).
map it
move the pointer into the netscape window
	(netscape - legible; all else - illegible)	<-- expected
move the pointer into another window
	(netscape - illegible; all else - legible)	<-- expected
move the pointer into the netscape window
	(netscape - legible; all else - illegible)	<-- expected
move the pointer into an embedded gif and/or a hot-link
	(netscape - illegible; all else - illegible)	<-- BUG <--<--<--<--
move the pointer into another window
	(netscape - illegible; all else - legible)	<-- expected
kill netscape.  Wait for Digital UNIX 4.0, or Mosaic 2.8b1 ;-)

This does not happen on any other X server I've tried Netscape on 
(the 3.2C X server running on AlphaStations with ZLXp-e1 cards, DEC
3000/{3,4,5}00s with the 8-bit TC graphics cards, or any Suns or
DECstations with 8-bit cards running netscape remotely from an alpha).
Hence, I assume its a bug in the ATI Mach64 X server as shipped with
3.2C.

