In article <beaudrie-0206961254370001@mic1.dom.uab.edu> beaudrie@oberon.cmc.uab.edu (Ken Beaudrie) writes:

>   I just ... took over an Alphaserver 2100 running Digital Unix 3.2 ... I've
>been selectivly upgrading software. The C++ version was 1.32 so I upgraded to
>5.3. Since then I am told that make doesn't understand files with .cpp, .cxx,
>.cc, etc. extensions. Doing a "make -p -f /dev/null" shows that, sure enough,
>those extensions are not recognized. Should they be? Did I do something wrong
>during the install?

The 3 ``make''s we ship don't yet know about DEC C++.  (It's on our agenda to
teach them about it).


>Is there something I should do that is not listed in the install document
>for C++? Any help would be greatly appreciated.

In the meantime, users can add lines like the following to their makefiles, or
to a file ``Makeconf'':

"
CXX = /usr/ucb/cxx
CXXFLAGS = $(CFLAGS)

.SUFFIXES : .cxx
.cxx.o : ; $(CXX) $(CXXFLAGS) -c $< -o $@
.cxx : ; $(CXX) $(LDFLAGS) $(CXXFLAGS) $< $(LOADLIBES) -o $@
"

Say ``man make'' for details about placement of Makeconf.
Say ``man 1p make'' and ``man 1u make'' for details on the other two ``make''s.
Fetch ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/GNU/make-3.74.tar.gz for the Free Software
Foundation's GNU Make.
				Hope this helps,
				/AHM
