X-Deja-AN: 158574738
references: <4ofk0t$svi@jeeves.niehs.nih.gov>
organization: Compulink Information eXchange
x-news-software: Ameol
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Hi,

To check out the amount of swapspace in use, enter the following command:

# swapon -s

This will give a summary of swap space usage.

One thing that is commonly done is to remove the file /sbin/swapdefault 
(This is a symbolic link to the swap device file).

If the system doesn't see this file, it enters a "lazy" mode, meaning 
that it only allocates swap space for a process when it needs it. The 
default "eager" mode allocates sufficient swap space for a process when 
it starts up. There is a possibility that processes will get killed off 
by the system if it is in lazy mode though.

Another desirable thing is to back up your user disk (if it has enough 
space), move partitions around (with disklabel) so that there is a 
partition with a couple of hundred Megabytes on it. You can then edit the 
fstab and copy the entry for the original swapfile with a new one. 
Digital UNIX likes to have two swapfiles to play with.

Mark Jeffery



