In article <31AD36CB.558@cao.mts.dec.com> Peter Mayne <peter.mayne@cao.mts.dec.com> writes:

>Why don't you just mount the CDs on a system somewhere and share them using NFS?
>
>The Layered Products CD is just a CD with a normal filesystem.
>The Firmware CD is an ISO9660 CD, but once it's mounted using the right 
>parameters, it looks just like any other CD.
>The UNIX CD is just another CD.
>
>And before you say "but I need to put the UNIX CD into the drive to do an 
>install", set things up on the system with the CD so you can install (and 
>upgrade firmware) using bootp. The instructions are in the firmware release 
>notes and the UNIX installation guide.

As far as the Layered products are concerned we are already using NFS
and will continue doing so. No big problem there. One minor problem
tough is that bootp doesn't work trough routers (there are significant
topological network distances between the different departments that
use Digital Unix, but this can easily be circumvented bu setting up
local install boot servers.

The second alternative is actually exactly what we would like to do
but we can't do that efficiently because of some bug in DUnix (which I
btw have reported) which doesn't allow me to change bootptab entries
on a server. Let's say that I set up a bootp/tftp server to deliver
the firmware update to a client. Works like a charm. Now I want to
change the entry to point to another kernel for the same machine. To
do this I simply change the entry in /etc/bootptab, kill all possible
running bootpd:s. This is what I have done lots of times on other
systems (Solaris, HP-UX, AIX and others) and there it works. On DUnix
(we run 3.2d-1) it doesnt. The machine will stubbornly continue to
deliver the first information entered even if I reconfigure inetd not
to run bootpd. The only way to change seems to be a server reboot.

Thanks for your time
/Erik


































































